|~ `::error`, the kind every error is a value of, and what is declared
   under it: the refusal of an error whose path is no vector of stops,
   the facts a refusal may carry, and the words of errors. The error at
   rest, the value `!|` catches a failure to, is `::error{…}`, or the tag
   of the site that refused stacked over it, and its walk passes
   `::error` on its way to the map, so a word declared here reaches every
   error. ~|

:error
  |~~ An error is the facts of a failure as a value: what refused, on
      what, and the path the failure took, under the tag of the site
      that refused, which names the page that tells how to repair the
      call. A step that fails answers an error raised, a failure; a
      reader learning a tool fails often, and the failure brings what
      the reader needs at the moment it is cheapest to give.

      An error at rest is written with a bang, `!{:reason :late}`, the
      tag of its site before the bang, `::Late!{:by 3}`; it is
      `::error{…}` under that tag, and prints with its bang, as a set
      prints with its hash. It is a value like any other: a container
      holds it, its facts read as fields, `!{:reason :late} | /reason`
      answering `:late`, and JSON writes it as a value under its tag.

      Raised by `raise`, an error is a failure, which lives in the pipe
      alone; a host prints it as its error at rest under the line
      `|~| raised, and no !| caught it`, which a reading passes over, so
      the print reads back as the error at rest, as `!|` grounds it.
      `|` passes a failure by, writing its path, and `!|` alone catches
      it, its step running on the error at rest: `"x" | add 1 !| type`
      answers the site, `::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError`, and
      `trail` the path. No value holds a failure, so a literal, a name
      or a fact whose part fails answers the failure.
      ::snippet~(::error | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::explanation | doc) ::link~(::fail | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|

:error/writesAnErrorAtRestByItsBang
  |~~ The bang writes an error at rest of the kind of errors. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:detail "boom"} | type | eq ::error)

:error/standsOverItsKind
  |~~ Beneath the site's tag lies an `::error`. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| payload | type | eq ::error)

:error/readsItsFacts
  |~~ An error's facts read as fields. ~~|
  ::law~(::error{:k 1} | /k | eq 1)

:error/takesAFactOnTheWay
  |~~ A fact added on the fail track rides on. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| union {:request "r-1"} | raise !| /request | eq "r-1")

:error/recoversByItsType
  |~~ A failure of a known type recovers. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError) ~(0) ~(raise) | eq 0)

:error/raisesAgainOtherwise
  |~~ A failure of another type raises again as itself. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| if (type | eq ::Other) ~(0) ~(raise) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:error/TrailNotVecError
  |~~ `:trail` among the facts of an error is its path, a
      vector of stops the runtime writes, `{:step :subject :skipped}`,
      from the step where it arose to the step where it is read.
      A `raise` of a map that writes a value that is no plain vector
      under `:trail`, a set, a quote and a doc among them, is refused as
      it makes the error. A `raise`
      that writes the `:trail` it read goes on with that path, and one
      that leaves it out starts a path at its own step.
      ::link~(::error | doc)
  ~~|

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesANumberForATrail
  |~~ A number given as a trail is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops :trail 5} | raise !| [type /actualType] | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::number])

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesATrailLaidOverAFailure
  |~~ A trail of a number laid over an error is refused when it is raised. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops} | raise | count !| union {:trail 5} | raise !| type | eq ::error/TrailNotVecError)

:error/TrailNotVecError/startsATrailAtTheRaise
  |~~ An error raised without a trail starts its trail at the raise. ~~|
  ::law~(!{:kind :oops} | count !| minus #[:trail] | raise | add 1
    !| /trail | map ~/step | eq [~(raise)])

:error/TrailNotVecError/refusesASetAQuoteAndADoc
  |~~ A set, a quote and a doc are no plain vector of stops. ~~|
  ::law~([#[1] ~(a) |~~ d ~~|] | map ~({:trail /} | raise !| type) | eq [::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError ::error/TrailNotVecError])

:error/name
  |~~ The name a reader asked for, a keyword or a tag, where nothing answers it. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::tag]

:error/addresses
  |~~ The addresses where a word of that name lives, one for each kind that answers it. ~~|
  ::set

:error/nearest
  |~~ The names nearest to it in spelling. ~~|
  ::vec

:error/index
  |~~ The place of the element refused, counted from zero. ~~|
  ::number

:error/actualType
  |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/places
  |~~ How many places the parts of the word read, where the call gave
      more values.
  ~~|
  ::number

:error/actualValue
  |~~ The value refused, where it is not the subject that the first stop
      of the trail holds.
  ~~|
  ::any

:error/leftType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the left. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/rightType
  |~~ The kind of the value on the right. ~~|
  ::tag

:error/field
  |~~ The field that does not fit, null when the payload is no map. ~~|
  #[::keyword ::null]

:error/printed
  |~~ The text a value prints as, its literal: a step that reads back as
      another step or as none, or an answer the JSON channel has no form for.
  ~~|
  ::string

:error/trail
  |~~ Answers the path of the error, a vector of stops from the step
      that raised it to the `!|` that opened it, each a map of the
      `:step`, the `:subject` that step met, and the steps `:skipped`
      after it: `"x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail` answers the one stop of
      `add 1`, which met `"x"` and skipped `mul 2`. A step that ran the
      step that raised adds its stop after it, the `map` that ran an
      element among them, so the path reads outward:
      `[1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map ~/step` answers
      `[~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))]`. An error at rest that flew no step
      holds no `:trail` and answers `[]`, `::error{:a 1} | trail`. The
      subject is an error at rest, the value `!|` catches a failure to:
      under `|` a failure passes `trail` by, as it passes every step, and
      a value that is no error is refused, a map with the key `:trail`
      among them.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc) ::link~(::error | doc)
  ~~|
  ~(at :trail | coalesce [])

:error/trail/answersNothingForAnErrorThatFlewNoStep
  |~~ An error that flew no step holds no path. ~~|
  ::law~(::error{:a 1} | trail | eq [])

:error/trail/answer
  |~~ The stops of the path the error took. ~~|
  ::vec

:error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError
  |~~ `trail` reads the path of an error at rest, the value `!|`
      catches a failure to, `"x" | add 1 !| trail`; the key `:trail` of a
      plain map is read by `/trail`.
  ~~|

:error/trail/holdsTheStepThatRaised
  |~~ The trail of an error raised at once holds the one step. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| trail | count | eq 1)

:error/trail/meetsTheSubjectOfTheStep
  |~~ The stop holds the subject the step met. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| trail | first | /subject | eq "x")

:error/trail/namesTheStepsSkipped
  |~~ The stop of the step that raised names the steps skipped after it. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 | mul 2 !| trail | first | /skipped | eq ~(mul 2))

:error/trail/readsOutward
  |~~ The step that ran an element adds its stop after the element's. ~~|
  ::law~([1 "x"] | map ~(mul 2) !| trail | map ~/step | eq [~(mul 2) ~(map ~(mul 2))])

:error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError/refusesAMap
  |~~ A map with the key `:trail` is no error. ~~|
  ::law~({:trail []} | trail !| [type /actualType] | eq [::error/trail/SubjectNotErrorError ::map])
